1
00:00:00,256 --> 00:00:01,543
Hi, I'm Derric.

2
00:00:01,664 --> 00:00:02,589
This is Gridline.

3
00:00:02,773 --> 00:00:03,456
Let me show you

4
00:00:03,456 --> 00:00:06,176
How I'd use it

5
00:00:06,176 --> 00:00:07,716
to check a wind forecast

6
00:00:07,716 --> 00:00:09,516
against what actually happened.

7
00:00:09,516 --> 00:00:12,056
the dashboard covers decisions

8
00:00:12,056 --> 00:00:14,556
made during January

9
00:00:14,856 --> 00:00:15,034
twenty

10
00:00:15,112 --> 00:00:15,742
twenty-six.

11
00:00:15,931 --> 00:00:17,773
A decision on one day is matched with

12
00:00:17,766 --> 00:00:19,608
generation on the following day.

13
00:00:19,899 --> 00:00:22,210
Right now, the selected cutoff is three thirty

14
00:00:22,288 --> 00:00:23,981
in the afternoon, London time.

15
00:00:24,208 --> 00:00:25,858
I'll pick the tenth of January.

16
00:00:26,086 --> 00:00:27,893
The delivery date changes to the eleventh.

17
00:00:28,065 --> 00:00:30,760
The green line is the forecast available at

18
00:00:30,923 --> 00:00:31,784
decision time.

19
00:00:31,948 --> 00:00:34,707
The dashed line is the actual generation.

20
00:00:35,019 --> 00:00:37,118
You can see that the forecast is consistently

21
00:00:37,238 --> 00:00:38,003
higher here.

22
00:00:38,198 --> 00:00:40,509
If I open the values underneath, I can

23
00:00:40,502 --> 00:00:43,069
check the actual numbers and when that forecast

24
00:00:43,104 --> 00:00:43,944
was published.

25
00:00:44,171 --> 00:00:46,162
That publication time matters.

26
00:00:46,390 --> 00:00:48,530
A later revision can't be used to judge

27
00:00:48,566 --> 00:00:49,748
an earlier decision.

28
00:00:50,020 --> 00:00:52,374
The monthly mean absolute error is about eighteen

29
00:00:52,366 --> 00:00:55,616
hundred and seventy-three megawatts, and the bias is

30
00:00:55,652 --> 00:00:56,406
positive.

31
00:00:56,761 --> 00:00:59,798
So in this snapshot, forecasts generally sit above

32
00:00:59,876 --> 00:01:00,748
the actuals.

33
00:01:01,008 --> 00:01:04,045
These are forecast-error measures, rather than a calculation

34
00:01:04,251 --> 00:01:05,645
of money saved.

35
00:01:06,043 --> 00:01:08,866
To find the days worth investigating, I go

36
00:01:08,858 --> 00:01:10,188
to Daily accuracy.

37
00:01:10,437 --> 00:01:12,834
The tenth of January stands out, with an

38
00:01:12,869 --> 00:01:15,693
average error of roughly forty-eight hundred and eighty-one

39
00:01:15,685 --> 00:01:16,488
megawatts.

40
00:01:16,679 --> 00:01:18,819
I can open that row and go straight

41
00:01:18,855 --> 00:01:20,377
back to its chart.

42
00:01:20,732 --> 00:01:22,958
The date selection also handles the end of

43
00:01:22,951 --> 00:01:25,902
the month: the thirty-first of January maps to

44
00:01:25,895 --> 00:01:27,843
delivery on the first of February.

45
00:01:28,156 --> 00:01:30,254
One detail I wanted to make clear is

46
00:01:30,289 --> 00:01:31,461
the time resolution.

47
00:01:31,723 --> 00:01:33,779
On Method and sources, you can see that

48
00:01:33,771 --> 00:01:37,021
the forecasts are hourly, while the actual observations

49
00:01:37,227 --> 00:01:38,557
are half-hourly.

50
00:01:38,891 --> 00:01:41,800
The app averages both half-hours for an hourly

51
00:01:41,920 --> 00:01:42,717
comparison.

52
00:01:43,030 --> 00:01:45,947
It doesn't measure half-hour forecast accuracy.

53
00:01:46,204 --> 00:01:48,473
The two datasets also aren't proven to cover

54
00:01:48,594 --> 00:01:50,244
exactly the same wind assets.

55
00:01:50,471 --> 00:01:53,294
Underneath, it's a React frontend with a Fast

56
00:01:53,372 --> 00:01:56,338
A P I backend reading archived Elexon records.

57
00:01:56,559 --> 00:01:59,596
The important safeguard is selecting only forecast publications

58
00:01:59,844 --> 00:02:01,260
available by the cutoff.

59
00:02:01,551 --> 00:02:04,246
The existing Python and CSV checks passed during

60
00:02:04,324 --> 00:02:05,038
validation.

61
00:02:05,268 --> 00:02:07,665
That's Gridline: I can start with the monthly

62
00:02:07,700 --> 00:02:10,481
picture, find a difficult day, and inspect the

63
00:02:10,474 --> 00:02:13,033
